## Context
Follow-up to the author's review of the in-progress Git-notes implementation (tracker 1252).
Three defects were reproduced on main bf0db9ec55b4edc974556b35563cff7730ff3650 in temporary
repositories with a local bare remote. These concern delivered C3 behaviour, not the still-open
gate/raid migration or the pending web-environment trials. Do not redesign the accepted store.

Baseline validation: tests/test_review_notes.py, tests/test_gaze_branch.py, tests/test_forge.py,
tests/test_raid_land_local.py and tests/test_after_merge.py: 79 passed. The scenarios below are
additional probes, not existing failing tests. The snapshot matters: coordinate with the active
1274 work (PR #1813) and recheck against current main before implementation.

## Reproductions

### 1. Publication claims retrievability when retention refs were rejected (highest priority)
Use the two-clone world fixture in tests/test_review_notes.py. Install an executable update hook
in the bare origin that exits 1 for refs/review-heads/* and 0 for other refs. Open a review on
feature against origin/main, then publish its request entry id.

Observed: publish returns retrievable and the request exists in refs/notes/review, but
git ls-remote origin 'refs/review-heads/*' is empty. sync ignores the retention-ref push result
(scripts/review_notes.py:319-320); publish checks only the notes tip and entry ids (:327-336).

Expected: complete retrievability must not be claimed when the reviewed code's required retention
refs were not published and verified. Report missing, moved or rejected refs and preserve the
local entries and heads for retry. Otherwise later branch rewriting/deletion can leave readable
reports without their examined commits. Do not repair a moved remote ref by force-pushing it.

### 2. A bootstrapped clone fetches the notes but readers do not find the review
Clone A opens and publishes a review. In clone B, run review_notes.setup(B), then git fetch origin.
Inspect all_entries(B, TRACK), all_entries(B), and find(B, 'feature').

Observed: one entry in refs/notes/origin/review, zero in refs/notes/review, find returns None.
An explicit sync(B) repairs discovery, but the normal resolver does not perform it before reading.
gaze_setup.py:151-155 can therefore open a new review for the same branch instead of resuming it.

Expected: the documented fresh-clone bootstrap/resolution path integrates fetched review data
before deciding that no request exists, or explicitly refuses with an actionable sync requirement.
It must preserve local unpushed entries and distinguish missing data from a failed fetch. Ordinary
read/resolution should not unnecessarily require remote write permission; reader-only web access
is part of the accepted transport design.

### 3. A late answer is attached to the latest request instead of the request reviewed
In one clone, open request R1 on head H1. Advance feature and write request R2 on head H2.
Then call add('answer', review_id, old_run_fields, head=H1) for the execution launched on R1.

Observed: the answer correctly names H1 but receives R2's request_id, because add selects the
latest request at publication time (scripts/review_notes.py:430-440). This is reproducible without
actual process concurrency and models a reviewer finishing after a new submission.

Expected: freeze request identity when the execution starts and carry it through publication.
The answer must reference R1, or fail explicitly on an inconsistent binding; never silently bind
an old-head result to R2. Apply the same rule to plan/report entries and their links as appropriate.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Regression test: a remote accepting notes but rejecting required review-head refs cannot yield
      retrievable; the failure is visible and all local evidence remains available for retry.
- [ ] Publication verifies the required remote retention refs against their request heads; missing
      or moved refs are not silently accepted or force-overwritten.
- [ ] Regression test: a fresh second clone follows the documented bootstrap and discovers the
      existing review without creating a duplicate; a fetch failure is not treated as no review.
- [ ] Reader bootstrap preserves local unpublished entries and works without requiring a push.
- [ ] Regression test: a result from R1 arriving after R2 remains bound to R1 (or is explicitly
      refused); request/run identity is carried from setup to publication, not inferred as latest.
- [ ] Update affected helper/caller contracts and run the focused tests above, then the repository's
      required gate. Keep the forge path unchanged.

